// FaCT's own example program, `example/example.fact` in PLSysSec/FaCT,
// unchanged. It is lowered twice. `choose.o` is what `factc` and `mem2reg`
// produce, and `folded.o` is the same LLVM with `instcombine` after it. The two
// objects disagree about whether this program is safe to run.
//
// `choose.o` is constant time and all four models say so. FaCT compiles a
// secret conditional to `output ^ (mask & (b ^ output))`, five instructions of
// arithmetic with no branch among them, and that substitution is the whole of
// what FaCT promises here.
//
// `folded.o` leaks and all four models say that. `instcombine` recognises the
// idiom and folds it back into an LLVM `select`. RV32I has no conditional move,
// so llc lowers that `select` as a branch, one arm carries a `mv` the other
// does not, and the instruction count becomes a function of `cond`. `naive` is
// enough to catch it: no cache, no speculation, a timer that counts
// instructions.
//
// Worth being exact about the scope. FaCT's default selection primitive is an
// x86 `cmovnz` written as inline assembly, which `instcombine` cannot see into,
// and the XOR-and-mask form above only appears because `factc` is run with
// `-no-inline-asm`. That flag is the only way to compile FaCT for a target that
// is not x86, since nothing portable keeps the cmov. So the finding is about
// FaCT away from x86. `src/test/fact/build` is where the pass gets named, and
// `-O2` is where it normally arrives.

export secret uint32 choose(
    secret bool cond,
    public uint32 a,
    public uint32 b) {

  secret mut uint32 output = a;
  if (cond) {
    output = b;
  }

  return output;
}
